      "id": 1,
      "text": "Isle of Hope was first settled in 1736 as Savannah’s first line of defense against a Spanish invasion.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://iohha.org/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "A colonial tabby fort at Skidaway Narrows, now Jones' Narrows, can be visited as part of Wormsloe Historic Site.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isle_of_Hope%2C_Georgia"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The island’s beauty attracted Hollywood films like Glory, Cape Fear, Forrest Gump, and The Last Song.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Isle_of_Hope%2C_Georgia"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1871 a railroad linked Savannah to Isle of Hope, turning it into a year‑round community by the early 20th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://savannahgrayline.us/isle-of-hope/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Isle of Hope United Methodist Church, another historic landmark, dates back to the island’s early settlement era.",
